es with aggregate annual figures are evidenced","Ongoing adviser fee stated as 'circa £1,101.85 for the first year' calculated as '1% per annum' but the basis and whether this 1% applies to fund value (£110,185 would yield £1,101.85) or to contributions is not explicit in the report quote; no standalone adviser services fee schedule is presented in evidence","Report states £3,305.55 initial fee is 3% of 'amount invested' with 'current transfer values', but evidence shows transfer values total £116,998.47 (not £110,185); 3% of £116,998 would be £3,509.94, not £3,305.55; the fee basis is therefore internally inconsistent with the stated calculation","Cashflow tables show annual surplus figures (£24,001 in 2025, £23,886 in 2026) but do not itemise monthly breakdown to test the 'approximately £1,700 per month' expenditure claim; annual equivalent would be ~£20,400 expenditure (if £24,000 annual surplus on £41,813 income), not £1,700 × 12 = £20,400 which would require income of ~£44,400, but evidence shows £41,813 total"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["Itemised monthly or annual income sources (employment income, pension income, investment income) — only aggregate 'Total Money In' figures provided","Itemised monthly or annual expenditure breakdown — only aggregate 'Total Regular Expenditure' provided in charts without numeric detail","Standalone adviser ongoing service fee schedule or contract confirmation of £1,101.85 basis — only report assertion and passing reference in illustration","Emergency fund target or adequacy assessment — report references 'medium capacity for loss' but does not specify emergency reserve amount or duration","Reconciliation of pension transfer values (£116,998.47 evidence vs £110,185 stated in proposed plan) and corresponding fee calculation"],"analysis":"The comparison can be performed on the core affordability test using the evidence available. (1) Initial commitment: Report asserts £3,305.55 as 3% of 'amount invested'. Evidence shows transfer values summing to £116,998.47, but the proposed scenario plan shows new Aviva pension value of £110,185 (transfers of £3,744 + £103,439 + £3,002 = £110,185). If 3% applies to £110,185, the fee is £3,305.55, which matches the report. However, the extraction notes flag this as internally inconsistent across the document (£110k, £106k, £116k variants cited). The guard permits one-off transfer fees to be excluded from ongoing affordability testing; initial transfer fees are not affordability commitments per the rules. (2) Ongoing commitment: Report states £1,101.85 p.a. as 1% adviser fee. Evidence confirms platform + investment charges of £484.81 p.a. on the Aviva platform. The £1,101.85 figure, if 1% of £110,185, would represent adviser fees separate from platform charges. However, the evidence does not separately itemise an adviser ongoing fee of £617.04 p.a. (£1,101.85 − £484.81). The extraction notes cite this as an inconsistency: page 17 states 'circa £1,101.85 for the first year' under ongoing services, but this conflicts with the documented platform charges of £484.81. (3) Affordability basis: Report asserts income 'comfortably meets' expenditure of ~£1,700/month. Cashflow evidence shows annual surplus in 2025 of £24,001 and 2026 of £23,886, with Total Money In of £41,813 and £40,703 respectively. Monthly income implied is ~£3,485 (£41,813÷12), with implied annual expenditure of ~£20,400 (if surplus is £24,001), suggesting ~£1,700/month. Liquid assets and accessible pensions remain stable at £122,997–£124,588 across the projection, rising thereafter, indicating no deficit pattern. The ongoing adviser fee of £1,101.85 (or even the documented £484.81 platform charges) represents <3% of the documented annual surplus (£24,001), well within stated surplus margin. (4) Emergency fund: Report references 'medium capacity for loss' and states 'any potential underperformance would not have a detrimental impact on day-to-day living', but does not quantify emergency reserves. Baseline scenario shows £6,000 in cash savings account; proposed scenario shows same £6,000 opening cash position maintained. No explicit depletion of emergency reserves is evidenced by the recommendation (transfers consolidate existing pensions; cash position preserved). (5) Liquidity: Proposed scenario shows accessibility via the New Aviva pension platform (standard drawdown access) and retained £6,000 cash reserve. No liquidity mismatch is apparent. The fees and ongoing commitments are quantified and supported by evidenced cashflow showing consistent surplus. However, a material discrepancy remains: the ongoing adviser fee of £1,101.85 stated in the report is not reconciled against, or disaggregated from, the £484.81 documented platform charges. The extraction also flags ambiguity in pension valuation (£110k vs £116k), which affects fee calculation verification. Despite these clarifications, the cashflow evidence robustly supports that income exceeds expenditure with a comfortable surplus (£24,001 in year 1), and the ongoing charges in the range £484–£1,101 p.a. are materially affordable from that surplus. The false-positive guard regarding fees taken from the fund applies: initial and ongoing adviser fees are deducted from investment amounts or charged separately, not from the monthly surplus.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"intelliflo planning proposed scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["2025","69 / 65","122,997","41,813","24,001","0"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"intelliflo planning Baseline scenario.md","category":"F","quote":"Liquid Assets | £6,000","cells":null},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Plan | Platform | Investment Management | Total Charge | Aviva Platform Personal | 0.21% | 0.22% | 0.44% | Pension | £231.39 | £253.43 | £484.81","cells":null},{"passageId":"P7","source":"intelliflo planning proposed scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Savings",