vestment 20-60% Shares) at 25.66% performance and 6.43% volatility. (P7) references a 'New solution charges comparison' document section but the actual charge figures and cost comparison are not fully quoted in the passages. The SIPP research report (P2, P3, P6) provides fund performance data for the recommended funds (CT Universal MAP Balanced at 44.73% 5-year return; HSBC Global Strategy Balanced at 40.06% 5-year return) showing performance 'including the effect of ongoing costs and charges' (P2). No passage quotes a comprehensive cost justification or benefit-versus-cost analysis that reconciles the claimed 'lower costs' benefit with the actual ongoing charges or initial fees.","discrepancies":["The report claims 'Lower Costs' as a primary benefit but no passage provides a detailed cost comparison showing the new plan is cheaper on an all-in basis (initial fees, ongoing platform charges, fund charges, and adviser fees combined) versus the existing arrangements.","The report asserts benefits of 'Adviser Charging Facilitation' and 'Ongoing Monitoring and Adjustments' as justifications for the switch, but the cost of these services (initial 3% fee = £3,305.55, ongoing 1% = £1,101.85 p.a. per the canonical model) is not explicitly reconciled against tangible client-specific benefits in the passages.","The performance comparison (P1, P4) shows the recommended Aviva portfolio did NOT outperform the existing Zurich holdings (40.02% vs 44.32% over 5 years), contradicting any implicit benefit claim that switching will deliver superior returns. The report acknowledges this but does not explain why lower cost justifies accepting lower performance.","The report cites 'Greater Investment Choice - a wider range of investment options' but provides no evidence of how this specific benefit applies to the client's circumstances or what materially different outcomes this enables versus the existing arrangements.","The report asserts 'Enhanced Retirement Flexibility - including tax-