cates initial fees of £3,305.55 and ongoing adviser fees of approximately £1,101.85 p.a., but these are not reconciled against existing arrangement costs in the evidence. (3) Qualitative benefits: The report lists qualitative benefits (adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring, investment choice, flexibility) but no passage contains analysis of how these benefits materially apply to this specific client or quantifies their value. The report does not explain, for example, how the ability to deduct adviser charges 'directly' from the new scheme creates material benefit versus separate payment from the existing scheme, or whether this client requires the stated 'ongoing monitoring' that justifies the ongoing fee commitment. (4) Fair value: PRIN 2A.4 requires that charges are fair and the recommendation is cost-justified. The absence of a comprehensive benefit-versus-cost analysis — showing either that lower costs justify the switch despite lower performance, or that enhanced benefits justify higher costs — means the fair value assessment cannot be verified against the evidence. The recommendation increases the client's annual cost burden (adding 1% adviser fee = £1,101.85 p.a.) on top of platform and fund charges; this cost increase is not reconciled against demonstrable benefits in the case file. The extraction metadata notes the assertion status is 'Stated' (not derived), but the supporting calculations appear incomplete in the pack.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"The recommended Aviva advised funds have demonstrated stronger performance compared to your existing Aviva holdings. However, they have not outperformed your current Zurich funds.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"In contrast, the Aviva platform funds selected for you align with your agreed risk tolerance and have delivered comparable performance with reduced volatility.","