{"groupId":"CHK-009","requirement":"[G9.5] Switching costs exceed the benefits — Appropriateness limb","reportSays":"The report asserts that switching costs do not exceed benefits. For the Aviva plan (£3,744.36), the report states annual charges are 0.06% cheaper (saving £2.25/year) with no exit penalties. For the Zurich plan (£103,439.24), annual charges are 0.49% cheaper (saving £506.85/year) with no exit penalties. For the Standard Life plan (£3,002.00), annual charges are 0.26% MORE expensive (cost increase of £268.94/year). The report cites lower costs, lifestyling risk drift correction, greater fund choice, adviser charging facilitation, ongoing monitoring, diversification improvements, and enhanced retirement flexibility as benefits. Initial advice fee of £3,305 is charged against the new solution.","fileSays":"The evidence presents switching cost-benefit analysis in three forms: (1) Before adviser charges: Aviva shows £2,000 (1.6%) decrease in projected value at mid-growth; Zurich shows £2,000 (1.6%) decrease; Standard Life shows £10 (0.3%) decrease. (2) After adviser charges included: Aviva shows £10,400 (9.6%) decrease; Zurich shows £12,000 (9.4%) decrease; Standard Life shows the comparison not provided with adviser charges. Reduction in Yield for Zurich increases from 0.3% (existing) to 0.6% (new solution) before adviser charges, and to 2.2% after adviser charges. Critical Yield for Zurich is 4.8% at mid-growth after adviser charges. The evidence states 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' when comparing Aviva plan specifically [P3]. Alternative comparisons show Aviva alone has effect of switching of £20 (0.5% decrease) and Standard Life alone has £10 (0.3% decrease) at mid-growth without adviser charges.","discrepancies":["Report asserts annual charge advantage of -0.06% for Aviva (saving £2.25); evidence shows at mid-growth the existing Aviva pension projects to £4,280 vs new solution £4,260, a net decrease of £20 or 0.5% over the period, not an advantage.","Report asserts annual charge advantage of -0.49% for Zurich (saving £506.85); evidence shows at mid-growth the existing Zurich pension projects to £127,000 vs new solution £115,000 (with adviser charges), a decrease of £12,000 or 9.4%, contradicting the claimed cost benefit.","Report does not disclose the full cost-benefit impact including adviser charges in the main analysis; the suitability report presents only annual charge percentages and amounts, not the critical yield thresholds (1.9%, 4.8%, 7.8%) or the substantial switching costs (£2,000–£15,000 decreases depending on growth scenarios and adviser charge inclusion).","Evidence [P3] explicitly states 'No benefits were listed for the existing products' for the Aviva plan comparison, yet the suitability report asserts multiple benefits of switching (lower costs, lifestyling correction, fund choice, etc.) without evidencing lost benefits from existing arrangements.","Report claims lower costs as primary benefit; evidence after adviser charges shows Reduction in Yield increasing from 0.3% to 2.1–2.3% (depending on growth rate), meaning the new solution has substantially HIGHER costs once adviser charges are included, not lower.","Critical Yield of 4.8% for Zurich plan at mid-growth [P3] indicates investment growth would need to exceed normal expectations to justify the switch; report does not disclose this required return threshold."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The requirement examines whether switching costs exceed benefits. The evidence and report can be compared directly because both contain cost-benefit projections. However, a critical asymmetry exists in how costs are presented. The canonical model extracts annual charge differentials (-0.06%, -0.49%, +0.26%) from the suitability report as stated facts. These suggest net annual savings for two of the three switched plans. However, the retrieved evidence [P3] presents the 'effect of advice charges' analysis, which incorporates the £3,305 initial adviser fee and 1% ongoing annual adviser charge. Under this fuller accounting, the projected maturity values show substantial decreases: Zurich falls from £127,000 to £115,000 (£12,000 or 9.4% decrease) at mid-growth. This is directly contradictory to the narrative of cost savings. The evidence distinguishes between 'before advice charges' (showing smaller adverse effects) and 'after advice charges' (showing large adverse effects). The suitability report, as reflected in the canonical model, does not appear to disclose the full after-advice-charges position or the Critical Yield thresholds (1.9%, 4.8%, 7.8%), which are the investment growth rates required to break even. The evidence states these explicitly but the canonical model does not extract them. For Zurich at mid-growth after adviser charges, the Critical Yield is 4.8%, meaning the fund would need to earn 4.8% net growth just to match the existing pension—a high bar. The report's claim that 'lower costs' justify switching is contradicted by the evidence showing that when adviser charges are included (which they will be, as stated in [P3] 'Initial adviser charge of £3,305.00 which is taken from the new solution'), the cost structure is substantially worse, not better. The 'non-financial benefits' guard permits justification by non-cost factors if argued explicitly. The report does assert non-cost benefits: lifestyling risk drift, fund choice, diversification, retirement flexibility, adviser monitoring. However, these are generic benefits without client-specific quantification. Critically, the evidence [P3] states 'No benefits were listed for the existing products,' meaning the report has not conducted a paired benefits analysis—it has not shown what specific benefits the client would lose by switching, making it impossible to weigh non-financial benefits against the documented financial costs. The largest switched plan by value is Zurich (£103,439.24, representing approximately 88% of switched assets), and for this plan the evidence shows a 9.4% reduction in projected value after adviser charges at mid-growth—a material adverse effect. This meets the severity criterion of 'the largest ceding plan by value has no adequate justification for the cost burden.'","citations":[{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"No benefits were listed for the existing products","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Initial adviser charge of £3,305.00 which is taken from the new solution, reducing the initial investment accordingly.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Existing pension","New solution","£127,000","£115,000","0.3%","2.2%","-","4.8%","-","£12,000 or 9.4% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Critical Yield is the growth rate required from the new solution to match the projected maturity value of the pension solution.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension","New solution","£127,000","£125,000","0.3%","0.6%","-","3.2%","-","£2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P7","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.94%)","Existing pension","New solution","£4,280","£4,260","0.6%","0.6%","-","2.9%","-","£20 or 0.5% decrease"]}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

