 despite transfer.","discrepancies":["Report claims 'Lower Costs' as a primary benefit, but evidence shows: Aviva saves only £2.25 p.a. (-0.06%), Zurich saves £506.85 (-0.49%), yet Standard Life costs £268.94 MORE (+0.26%) — a net cost increase for one of three plans being consolidated.","Report projects to age 75, but financial illustrations in P2-P6 show the new solution will be worth £2,000-£3,000 LESS at age 75 than existing arrangements across low, mid, and high growth scenarios, contradicting a 'lower costs' benefit narrative.","Report cites critical yield of 4.8% required to match existing plan value, but P2 and P4 show critical yields of 0.3% (low), 3.2% (mid), and 6.2% (high) — inconsistent figures.","Report asserts benefits outweigh costs, but the projected value tables (P2-P6) show switching results in a loss: at mid growth £2,000-£3,000 smaller fund at retirement despite claimed lower ongoing costs.","Annualised benefit from cost savings: Aviva £2.25 + Zurich £506.85 - Standard Life £268.94 = £240.16 total annual saving. Over 5.5 years to age 75, this totals ~£1,320 gross, yet projected maturity value shortfall is £2,000-£3,000, leaving a net loss of £680-£1,680 unexplained.","Report does not explicitly address that the new solution's higher reduction in yield (0.6% vs 0.3%) and the switch costs (including adviser fees, though report states no exit penalties) together exceed the annual charge savings."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison can be made because the evidence pack contains detailed charge comparisons (P2-P6) and annual charge differentials (report canonical model and P1). The three false-positive guards do not apply here: (1) the comparison is not at solution level but per-plan as required — each plan shows individual cost/benefit tradeoff; (2) the report does attempt to explain why benefits outweigh costs, but the evidence contradicts this claim; (3) the charge analysis is present. Discrepancy analysis: The report's headline benefit of 'Lower Costs' is only partially supported. While Aviva and Zurich show lower ongoing annual charges, the consolidated figures reveal a material problem. The pension switch report (P2, P4) explicitly projects to age 75 using identical growth assumptions and shows the new solution will deliver £2,000-£3,000 less at retirement across all three growth scenarios. This is a direct contradiction of the 'lower costs' benefit narrative — lower ongoing costs do not translate to higher retirement values if the initial transfer, platform change, and adviser fee structure result in a net drag on returns. The report's own critical yield analysis (P1: 4.8% required growth) conflicts with the switch report critical yields (P2-P6: 0.3-6.2% range depending on scenario). The reconciliation is unclear: if 4.8% is required to break even on advice charges, but the switch report shows 3.2% critical yield at mid-growth, the implication is that the advice charge is absorbed in the lower bound, but this is not explained. Standard Life presents a particular concern: the plan is 0.26% more expensive per annum (£268.94), yet it is still recommended as part of the consolidation. The report does not explain why switching to a more expensive arrangement is justified for this plan. The overall effect of switching appears to be value-destructive by £2,000-£3,000 over the 5.5-year horizon, which is inconsistent with the assertion that benefits outweigh costs. The report addresses cost comparison and alternatives (multiple consolidation scenarios shown in P6), but does not adequately explain the projection shortfall or why a cost-saving argument should prevail when the maturity value is lower.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Based on a mid-growth rate of 2.94%, including our advice fees, the recommended plan would need to grow by 4.8% per year up to age 75 in order to meet the same value as your current plan.","cells":null},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Mid growth rate – (2.95%)","Existing pension","£127,000","Reduction in yield","0.3%","New solution","£125,000","Reduction in yield","0.6%","Effect of switching","-","£2,000 or 1.6% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P3","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Zurich - Zurich pension only","Mid growth rate – (2.94%)","Existing pensions","£120,000","New solution","£117,000","Effect of switching","-","£3,000 or 2.5% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["All three plans consolidated","High growth rate – (5.88%)","Existing pension","£150,000","New solution","£147,000","Effect of switching","-","£3,000 or 2.0% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P5","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":null,"cells":["Standard Life - SL pension only","Mid growth rate – (2.98%)","Existing pension","£3,440","New solution","£3,430","Effect of switching","-","£10 or 0.3% decrease"]},{"passageId":"P6","source":"Pension switch report - updated.md","category":"G","quote":"Alternative comparisons","cells":null},{"passageId":"P8","source":"Zurich Policy Info.md","category":"E","quote":"There are no deductions taken from the current value if benefits are transferred or taken.","cells":null}],"severity":"High","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

